= La zona, gobernada

The strangest thing about winning is what stays behind. In the sierra you did not only fight — you governed, because a guerrilla that holds ground has to: the assemblies that judged disputes, the fields redistributed, the clinics, the literacy circles, the small rough democracy of people running their own lives at gunpoint's remove from the state. Now the guns are down and the amnesty is signed, and that improvised order is suddenly *legal* — the closest thing to a workers' and peasants' government that has existed on Mexican soil since Cárdenas, and the one place in the country where people already know what it feels like to be governed by themselves. What you do with it decides whether it is a museum or a model.

- @gz_modelo: Make it the model — pour everything into showing the whole country that the liberated zone works, that socialism is not a slogan but a functioning municipality. *(+socialist governance; the south becomes a showcase)*
- @gz_extender: Extend it quietly — replicate the ejido democracy and the assemblies across the south before the machine notices and moves to reabsorb it. *(+peasant base, +regional roots)*

@gz_modelo
on-arrival: {! Q.f_socialismo=(Q.f_socialismo||0)+2; Q.sup_peasants=(Q.sup_peasants||0)+6; Q.fe_voto=(Q.fe_voto||0)+8; Q.legitimidad_regimen-=4; Q.leyenda=(Q.leyenda||0)+2; !}

You turn the liberated zone into an argument the whole country can watch: the cameras come, and what they film is not chaos but a municipality where the land is worked in common and the decisions are made in the open and the caciques are simply *gone.* It is the most dangerous thing a Mexican government can face — not a guerrilla in the hills but a working example in the valley, proof in a single county that the thing the left has promised for fifty years can actually be built. People come from other states to see it. Some of them go home and start.

- @root: Continuar.

@gz_extender
on-arrival: {! Q.f_socialismo=(Q.f_socialismo||0)+1; Q.sup_peasants=(Q.sup_peasants||0)+8; Q.red_casillas=(Q.red_casillas||0)+8; Q.municipio_alcozauca=1; !}

You do not make a monument; you make a *pattern.* The assemblies, the common fields, the recallable authorities — you replicate them, village by village, county by county, faster than the machine can decide whether to co-opt or crush them, until the south is threaded with small functioning pieces of the thing you fought for. It is less spectacular and far harder to kill: you cannot bomb a method, and the PRI cannot buy back a hundred villages that have learned to govern themselves. The roots go down. The machine will spend the next decade trying to pull them up, and finding that they have spread.

- @root: Continuar.

