= Los que bajaron

They came down out of the sierra with you, and now they are among the cadres — the ones who spent years being hunted and did not break, holding the posts where a person needs to have already seen the worst: security, the campo organizing, the places the soft political recruits cannot go. They are the movement's spine now, and its conscience, and occasionally its problem: men and women who took a provincial capital do not always take easily to committee meetings and the patience of legal work. The legend they carry organizes the south for a generation. The impatience they carry has to be given somewhere to go.

- @vet_cuadros: Make them the movement's backbone — put the veterans where their discipline and their legend do the most, and let the war's hardest lessons train the next generation. *(+unity, +security, +organizing muscle)*
- @vet_memoria: Make them its memory — let them build the archive, name the dead, teach the young why, so the victory is never sentimentalized into a story that forgets its cost. *(+legend, the counter-history secured)*

@vet_cuadros
on-arrival: {! Q.unity=(Q.unity||0)+8; Q.f_socialismo=(Q.f_socialismo||0)+1; Q.sup_peasants=(Q.sup_peasants||0)+4; Q.organizers=(Q.organizers||0)+3; !}

You put the veterans where iron is needed, and the movement gains something no amount of legal organizing could buy: a core that has already been tested by the worst the state can do and did not fold. They train the young cadres the way Bayo trained them — hard, and in habits, and with the authority of people who have actually done it. The soft parts of the movement grow a hard center. When the machine comes with money or with threats, it finds a spine it cannot bend, because the people holding it have already refused worse.

- @root: Continuar.

@vet_memoria
on-arrival: {! Q.leyenda=(Q.leyenda||0)+4; Q.memorial=(Q.memorial||0)+2; Q.f_socialismo=(Q.f_socialismo||0)+1; Q.fe_voto=(Q.fe_voto||0)+4; !}

You set the veterans to the work of memory, which a victorious movement neglects at its peril: the archive assembled, the dead named with their dates and their villages, the young taught not a sanitized legend but the real thing — what it cost, who paid, what was nearly lost and why. A revolution that remembers accurately is one that cannot be flattered into repeating its mistakes or shamed into forgetting its victories. The counter-history is secured in the one place the state can never raid: the trained memory of the people who lived it.

- @root: Continuar.

