face-image: img/Zocalo1988.jpeg
subtitle: The movement has real power in the south now — the hardest question is what kind
tags: event
view-if: (f_negociado = 1 or u_negociado = 1) and (via = "politica" or via = "institucional") and f_socialismo >= 3 and time >= f_gobierno_last + 4
priority: 5
max-visits: 1
on-arrival: f_gobierno_last = time

= El poder, y qué hacer con él

It has crept up almost unnoticed under the weight of daily work: the movement has *power* now — real, territorial, governing power in the south, won by the gun and legitimized by the accord and rooted in a hundred self-governing villages. This is the moment every revolution reaches and few survive with their soul intact, because power is the acid test that dissolved most of the left that ever touched it. The war was the easy part. The war had an enemy. This has only choices, and they are the ones that decide what all of it was *for.*

- @cam_socialismo: **La vía al socialismo.** Build it out — socialize the land and the key enterprises of the region, deepen the assembly democracy, make the south a functioning socialist polity within a capitalist state and dare the country to notice. *(the socialist-governance path; bold, and it invites the whole weight of reaction)*
- @cam_frente: **El frente amplio.** Don't overreach — hold the gains, build the broadest possible front, trade some of the radicalism for the durability that keeps the machine from crushing what you built. *(safer, broader, slower; the reformist synthesis)*
- @cam_nacional: **De lo regional a lo nacional.** Take the model national — use the southern base and the legend as the launch-pad for a genuine bid for national power in '88. *(ambition; ties the guerrilla victory to the electoral endgame)*

@cam_socialismo
on-arrival: {! Q.f_socialismo=(Q.f_socialismo||0)+4; Q.sup_peasants=(Q.sup_peasants||0)+8; Q.sup_workers=(Q.sup_workers||0)+5; Q.legitimidad_regimen-=8; Q.intervencion_riesgo=(Q.intervencion_riesgo||0)+8; Q.leyenda=(Q.leyenda||0)+3; !}

You choose the hardest road and the only one that would make the dead nod: you build the thing itself. The land of the region is socialized, the key enterprises brought under the assemblies, the rough wartime democracy deepened into a functioning system — a piece of actual socialism, governing actual lives, inside a state that cannot decide whether to tolerate it or destroy it. It is the boldest thing the Mexican left has done since the Revolution, and it summons every force that exists to stop such things: the machine, the army it did not fully dismantle, and the cold attention of a giant to the north for whom a working socialism on its border is the one thing more intolerable than a failed one. You have built the future. Now you have to defend it.

- @root: Continuar.

@cam_frente
on-arrival: {! Q.f_socialismo=(Q.f_socialismo||0)+1; Q.pri_reformist_rel=(Q.pri_reformist_rel||0)+10; Q.unity=(Q.unity||0)+8; Q.fe_voto=(Q.fe_voto||0)+8; Q.registro=(Q.registro<2?2:Q.registro); !}

You choose durability over daring. Instead of building an island of socialism to be drowned, you build a *front* — the broadest possible alliance, radical in aim and patient in method, that trades some of the maximalism for the one thing the Mexican left has never had: staying power. The gains in the south are held, not gambled; the movement becomes a permanent national factor rather than a heroic episode. Some of the veterans call it a betrayal of what the war was for. Some of them are right. And some of them will still be organizing, and free, and building, in twenty years — which is more than most revolutions can say.

- @root: Continuar.

@cam_nacional
on-arrival: {! Q.f_socialismo=(Q.f_socialismo||0)+2; Q.fe_voto=(Q.fe_voto||0)+14; Q.leyenda=(Q.leyenda||0)+4; Q.frente_unidad=(Q.frente_unidad||0)+10; Q.cd_formed=1; !}

You point all of it — the southern base, the governing example, the legend of the capital that fell — at the one prize that would change everything: national power, and soon. The movement stops being a regional miracle and becomes the anchor of a real bid for the presidency, the thing '88 could turn on. It is the highest-variance choice on the board: win it and the guerrilla war becomes the prologue to a government; lose it and you have bet the safest gains you will ever hold on the roll of an election the machine has never once lost cleanly. The dead in the sierra bought you this shot. Now you take it, for all of them.

- @root: Continuar.
